## Yes—provided the conditions of CAR 401.19 are met

Under **CAR 401.19(1)**, the **holder of a student pilot permit** may act as **pilot-in-command** on a **day VFR** flight **in Canada** **for the purpose of their flight training**, **if** the flight is conducted:

- **(a)** for the purpose of the holder’s flight training;  
- **(b)** in **Canada**;  
- **(c)** under **day VFR**;  
- **(d)** under the **direction and supervision** of a person qualified to provide training toward the permit/licence/rating for which the pilot-in-command experience is required; and  
- **(e)** **no passenger is carried on board**.

### Also allowed in an integrated course (day or night)
**CAR 401.19(2)** adds that if the student pilot permit holder is **enrolled in an integrated course**, they may act as pilot-in-command during a flight conducted under **day or night VFR** (the excerpt does not restate the “no passenger” condition in (2), but (1) sets the passenger requirement for the general rule).

✅ **So, for a daytime VFR training flight with no passengers on board:** it is permitted **as long as** the other CAR 401.19(1) conditions—especially **training purpose** and **qualified supervision**—are satisfied.
